course: dictionary
lesson: 7626
title: Words with steps and rules
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A procedure is a set of steps you follow in a certain order to do something correctly or safely. Think of a fire drill: everyone follows the same steps, in the same order, every time.
explain: Look for clues around the word. Words like «safety» and «emergency» nearby tell you the steps are there to keep people safe. «Fair procedures for sharing something scarce include drawing lots» tells you a procedure can also be a fair way of doing something.
explain: The same word can mean different things in different places. In a hospital, «a procedure» means an operation, a treatment a doctor carries out. In maths, a procedure is a method that works through a task step by step. Check the topic around the word, then put your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: A procedure is a set of steps to follow in a certain order to do something correctly or safely.
fact: In maths and computing, a procedure is a set of instructions that does a task, like a method.
fact: In hospitals, «a procedure» means an operation or surgery.
fact: Useful phrases: «follow the procedure», «safety procedures», «emergency procedure», «a simple procedure».
fact: Doctors may say «discomfort» to mean pain, and «an adverse event» for a mistake that harms a patient.
exercise: «Follow the procedure when the fire alarm rings.» What does procedure mean here? | *A set of steps to follow in order, but only for teachers | A type of fire engine | A loud noise made by the alarm | A set of steps to follow in order
exercise: In hospital talk, what does «a procedure» mean? | A visit from a friend | A written test in class | A kind of hospital bed | *An operation or surgery
exercise: Play specialists use dolls, pictures and games to show children what will happen and to distract them during procedures. Find one word that means to take someone's attention away from something. | pictures | games | dolls | *distract
test: «You may feel discomfort during a procedure.» What does discomfort mean in this sentence? | Sleepiness | *Pain | A cold drink | Happiness
test: In maths, what is a procedure? | A pile of numbers with no order | *A set of instructions that does a task, like a method | A coloured pencil | A drawing of a shape
exam: When you meet a new word like procedure, what is a good way to check your meaning? | Ignore the word and guess the answer | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense | Skip the sentence and never come back | Look only at the first word of the sentence
